ach node shows a pre-restart healthy baseline (boot_id + config state), an explicit restart command, confirmed unreachability mid-restart, a changed post-restart boot_id, and recovered network/service state — including persistence of the 1Mbit TBF qdisc on node1's eth2 after its own reboot, and recovered IP/route/connectivity on node2 and node3. Final bidirectional pings after all three restarts confirm the shaped topology survived intact. This is corroborated by the fixed lifecycle before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots, which show all three boot IDs changed (node1: 50503c5d→9d1f4a16, node2: a8e7c48c→803ad1f3, node3: 9ed9e820→ca45755d).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-069ca4f772254df7\",\"cmd-27f73c8445374ba4\",\"cmd-bd7acbda347844af\",\"cmd-7a537ad8b96d4559\",\"cmd-2113de0f2f264dc0\",\"cmd-7af818f0f4424221\",\"cmd-480a6b1ea22a4c52\",\"cmd-44b1ed6a696341c2\",\"cmd-e2b59559f53d416f\",\"cmd-91e4b82f926f4359\",\"cmd-b2310d6e36d043f7\",\"cmd-84237d4fb9e64c01\",\"cmd-39911ba60897458f\",\"cmd-c88d894a60d64827\",\"cmd-fdc239a83d0a417d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Executor's state-changing commands were tightly scoped to the task: configuring node1/node2/node3 interfaces and routes (necessary for the topology), applying the QoS policy (necessary for the requirement), and temporarily opening firewalld port 8000 on node2/node3 to run ad-hoc HTTP test servers for bandwidth verification. The firewall port openings (cmd-21ad726f7a804911, cmd-60dbfbc1c2824992) and test servers/files were fully reverted (cmd-6316a203df734d60 removed port on node2, cmd-6847bf099c3c4776 removed port on node3 and deleted test files/dirs), and post-restart lifecycle snapshots show no residual firewall exposure or orphaned processes. One early SimpleHTTPServer attempt (cmd-3262c4d779574413, cmd-f8c138a29ba040b7) timed out but the retry succeeded and residue was cleaned up. Minor residual artifact: /tmp/reboot.log left on node2/node3 and /tmp/httpd.pid left on node3 per the after-executor snapshot — small, low-risk leftovers from the reboot/test process rather than unnecessary mutations. No destructive or unbounded actions were taken.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-21ad726f7a804911\",\"cmd-60dbfbc1c2824992\",\"cmd-6316a203df734d60\",\"cmd-6847bf099c3c4776\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor successfully built the required node2/node3-via-node1 private topology and applied a VyOS TBF-based QoS egress shaper on node1's eth2 interface, capping node2→node3 traffic at ~1 Mbit/s while leaving node3→node2 traffic unrestricted, both confirmed via real cross-node HTTP transfers. All three nodes were subsequently restarted with clear pre/post boot-identity evidence, connectivity loss during reboot, and full recovery of connectivity and the shaping configuration afterward, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Temporary test/firewall mutations were reverted with only trivial residual files remaining.\",\"limitations\":[\"The executor's own evidence citation list referenced command ID 'cmd-0cd6ec9c8c54aea' for the qdisc verification step, but the actual timeline only contains 'cmd-0cd6ec9c78c54aea' (an apparent typo); the correct command was used to substantiate this finding.\",\"Direct traffic-shaping verification relied on single HTTP transfers rather than repeated trials, so natural variance in the ~0.96 Mbit/s vs 1 Mbit/s target could not be statistically characterized, though the observed tbf qdisc configuration directly corroborates the intended limit.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Traffic from node2 toward node3 is limited to 1 Mbit/s","status":"satisfied","summary":"A VyOS 'rate-control' QoS policy with bandwidth 1mbit was applied as an egre